${"##"} Input selection

Conditions: Every row holds in every cycle, except for a JTAG pad while a TAP owns it.

Testpoints: `pad_listen` (V1, priority 1), `reset_state` (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement | Sources | Checked by | Why the checks prove it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| REQ_IN_01 | MIO peripheral input `n` shall read 0 while `MIO_PERIPH_INSEL_n` is 0. | [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL.IN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel) | `pad_listen`: *reads tie 0* | In every cycle the check compares each MIO peripheral input whose select is 0 with 0. An input that reads a pad or 1 under select 0, even for one cycle, fails it. |
| REQ_IN_02 | MIO peripheral input `n` shall read 1 while `MIO_PERIPH_INSEL_n` is 1. | [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL.IN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel) | `pad_listen`: *reads tie 1* | In every cycle the check compares each MIO peripheral input whose select is 1 with 1. An input that reads a pad or 0 under select 1 fails it. |
| REQ_IN_03 | MIO peripheral input `n` shall read the input of MIO pad `p`, in the same cycle, while `MIO_PERIPH_INSEL_n` is 2 + `p` (`p` from 0 to ${n_mio_pads - 1}). | [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL.IN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_pads`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_listen`: *reads selected pad* | For each select from 2 to ${n_mio_pads + 1} the check compares the input with `mio_in_i` of pad select minus 2, in the same cycle. An input that reads another pad, or the right pad a cycle late, differs in some cycle and fails it. |
| REQ_IN_04 | MIO peripheral input `n` shall read 0 while `MIO_PERIPH_INSEL_n` is ${free(n_mio_pads + 2, insel_max)}, the values that name no pad. (*RTL*) | [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_pads`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_listen`: *no pad reads 0* | For selects ${free(n_mio_pads + 2, insel_max)}, which name no pad, the check compares the input with 0. An input that reads a pad there, for example by wrapping the select around, or reads 1, fails it. |
| REQ_IN_05 | DIO peripheral input `d` shall read the input of DIO pad `d`, in the same cycle, whatever the registers hold. | [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories) | `pad_listen`: *DIO reads its pad* | The check compares each DIO peripheral input with its own pad's `dio_in_i` in the same cycle, whatever the registers hold. An input that reads another DIO pad, depends on a register or lags by a cycle fails it. |
| REQ_IN_06 | After reset, every MIO peripheral input shall read 0 until software writes its select. | [Pinmux Configuration](../programmers_guide.md#pinmux-configuration); [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories); [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL.IN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel) | `pad_listen`: *reads tie 0* | The generated register checker keeps each input select at its reset value 0 until software writes it, and _reads tie 0_ (CHK_PAD_LISTEN_01) makes an input whose select is 0 read 0 in the same cycle. An input that reads 1 before its select is written fails one of the two. |

${"##"} Output selection

Conditions: Every row holds while the pad's sleep mode is not active (Sleep retention) and no TAP owns the pad.

Testpoints: `pad_output` (V1, priority 1), `reset_state` (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement | Sources | Checked by | Why the checks prove it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| REQ_OUT_01a | The output of MIO pad `p` shall be 0 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 0. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *tie 0 drives 0* | While an awake pad's select is 0, the check compares the pad's output with 0 in every cycle. A pad that puts 1 or a peripheral's data on its output there fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_01b | The output enable of MIO pad `p` shall be 1 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 0, so the pad drives 0. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *tie 0 drives 0* | Under select 0 the check compares the pad's output enable with 1 in every cycle. A pad left high-Z, or following a peripheral's enable, under select 0 fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_02a | The output of MIO pad `p` shall be 1 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 1. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *tie 1 drives 1* | While an awake pad's select is 1, the check compares the pad's output with 1 in every cycle. A pad that puts 0 or a peripheral's data on its output there fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_02b | The output enable of MIO pad `p` shall be 1 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 1, so the pad drives 1. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *tie 1 drives 1* | Under select 1 the check compares the pad's output enable with 1 in every cycle. A pad left high-Z, or following a peripheral's enable, under select 1 fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_03a | The output enable of MIO pad `p` shall be 0 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 2: the pad is high-Z. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *select 2 is high-Z* | While an awake pad's select is 2, the check compares the pad's output enable with 0 in every cycle. A pad that drives under select 2, even for one cycle, fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_03b | The output of MIO pad `p` shall be 0 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 2. | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *select 2 is high-Z* | Under select 2 the check compares the pad's output with 0 in every cycle. A pad that puts 1 or a peripheral's data on its output while high-Z fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_04a | MIO pad `p` shall carry peripheral output `k`, in the same cycle, while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 3 + `k` (`k` from 0 to ${n_mio_periph_out - 1}). | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel); [Muxing Matrix](../theory_of_operation.md#muxing-matrix); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_periph_out`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_output`: *drives selected output* | For each select from 3 to ${n_mio_periph_out + 2} the check compares the pad's output with `periph_to_mio_i[k]`, `k` the select minus 3, in the same cycle. A pad that carries another output's data, or the right data a cycle late, fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_04b | MIO pad `p` shall carry output enable `k`, in the same cycle, while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is 3 + `k` (`k` from 0 to ${n_mio_periph_out - 1}). | [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel); [Muxing Matrix](../theory_of_operation.md#muxing-matrix); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_periph_out`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_output`: *drives selected output* | The check compares the pad's output enable with `periph_to_mio_oe_i[k]` in the same cycle, together with the data. A pad that pairs one output's data with another output's enable, or lags the enable by a cycle, fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_05a | The output enable of MIO pad `p` shall be 0 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is ${free(n_mio_periph_out + 3, outsel_max)}, the values that name no peripheral output: the pad is high-Z. (*RTL*) | [`MIO_OUTSEL`](../registers.md#mio_outsel); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_periph_out`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_output`: *no output is high-Z* | For selects ${free(n_mio_periph_out + 3, outsel_max)}, which name no output, the check compares the pad's output enable with 0. A pad that drives there, for example by wrapping the select onto a real output, fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_05b | The output of MIO pad `p` shall be 0 while `MIO_OUTSEL_p` is ${free(n_mio_periph_out + 3, outsel_max)}, the values that name no peripheral output. (*RTL*) | [`MIO_OUTSEL`](../registers.md#mio_outsel); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_periph_out`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson) | `pad_output`: *no output is high-Z* | For selects ${free(n_mio_periph_out + 3, outsel_max)} the check compares the pad's output with 0. A pad that puts 1 or any output's data on its output there fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_06a | DIO pad `d` shall carry its peripheral's output, in the same cycle. | [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories) | `pad_output`: *DIO drives its peripheral* | The check compares each awake DIO pad's output with its own peripheral's, `periph_to_dio_i[d]`, in the same cycle, whatever the registers hold. A pad that carries another peripheral, depends on a register or lags by a cycle fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_06b | DIO pad `d` shall carry its peripheral's output enable, in the same cycle. | [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories) | `pad_output`: *DIO drives its peripheral* | The check compares each awake DIO pad's output enable with `periph_to_dio_oe_i[d]` in the same cycle, whatever the registers hold. A pad that takes another peripheral's enable, or lags by a cycle, fails it. |
| REQ_OUT_07 | After reset, every MIO pad shall be high-Z until software writes its select. | [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories); [Pinmux Configuration](../programmers_guide.md#pinmux-configuration); [`MIO_OUTSEL.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_outsel) | `pad_output`: *select 2 is high-Z* | The generated register checker keeps each output select at its reset value 2 until software writes it, and _select 2 is high-Z_ (CHK_PAD_OUTPUT_03) makes an awake pad that no TAP owns drive (0, 0) on select 2. A pad that drives before its select is written fails one of the two; an asleep pad and a JTAG pad a TAP owns are decided by the sleep and JTAG pad checks. |

${"##"} Pad attributes

Conditions: Every row holds in every cycle, asleep or awake, except on a JTAG pad while a TAP owns it.
"Written" means a write the register block accepts, its lock open (Register locks); "reads back" means what a read of the register returns.
A pad type "keeps" an attribute bit when its pads implement that bit ([Pad Attributes](../programmers_guide.md#pad-attributes)); which bits a type keeps is fixed, the same on every pad of the type and for good.
A one-bit field is kept when its bit is; each bit of `slew_rate` and `drive_strength` is kept or not on its own, as "certain pads may only support two drive-strength bits, instead of four" ([Pad Attributes](../programmers_guide.md#pad-attributes)).
Every pad keeps `invert` ([Generic Pad Wrapper](../theory_of_operation.md#generic-pad-wrapper)).

Testpoints: `pad_attr` (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement | Sources | Checked by | Why the checks prove it |
